 ],
 "literal": "Sailing ships on the vast, great ocean, / O descendant of the strong ancestor who commanded the action of the wind! / O Karikal Valavan with elephants of rutting nature! / Having gone and conquered in battle, so that your strength was manifest, / O victor! He is indeed better than you, / on the bustling, prosperous battlefield of Venni, / having attained a world of great fame, / he who sat facing north, ashamed of the wound on his back.",
 "translation": "O descendant of the strong one who commanded the wind's action to sail ships on the vast, deep ocean! O Karikāl Vaḷava, who possesses elephants of rutting nature! O victor, who went and conquered in battle so that your great strength was made manifest! He is indeed greater than you—the one who, on the bustling and prosperous battlefield of Veṇṇi, felt ashamed of the wound on his back and sat facing north, fasting to death, thereby attaining a world of supreme fame.",
 "modern": "பரந்த பெரிய கடலில் கப்பல்களைச் செலுத்தி, காற்றின் இயக்கத்தைக் கட்டுப்படுத்தி ஆண்ட வலிமைமிக்க முன்னோனின் வழித்தோன்றலே! மதம் பிடித்த இயல்பைக் கொண்ட யானைகளை உடைய கரிகால் வளவனே! போர்க்களத்திற்குச் சென்று போரிட்டு வென்று உன்னுடைய வலிமை வெளிப்படுமாறு செய்த வெற்றியாளனே! ஆரவாரம் மிக்க, புதிய வளங்களைக் கொண்ட வெண்ணிப் பறந்தலையில் (போர்க்களத்தில்), தன் முதுகில் பட்ட காயத்திற்காக நாணி, வடக்கு நோக்கி உயிர் துறக்க அமர்ந்தானே, அவன் உன்னை விடவும் நல்லவன் (சிறந்தவன்) அல்லவா? அவன் உலகப் புகழை அடைந்துவிட்டான்.",
 "explanation": "The poet Veṇṇik Kuyattiyār addresses the Chola king Karikāl Peruvaḷattāṉ after his victory at the battle of Veṇṇi. While she praises Karikālaṉ's illustrious lineage of seafaring ancestors who mastered the winds and his immense military strength, she boldly declares that the defeated Chera king, Perum Cēral Ātaṉ, is even greater. The Chera king, having received a wound on his back during the battle, felt deeply ashamed of this mark of dishonor. Consequently, he chose to perform 'vaṭakkiruttal' (fasting to death facing north) to reclaim his honor, thereby attaining eternal fame. This poem belongs to the 'vākai' tiṇai (victory), specifically 'araca vākai', but it beautifully subverts the praise of physical victory by highlighting the supreme moral victory of the defeated king.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
 "generated": "2026-11-26"
}